1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,011
Lesson 10.5.

2
00:00:02,391 --> 00:00:04,235
Encoding numbers in pbits.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:08,623
A digital chip stores numbers
with a fixed width of bits.

4
00:00:09,003 --> 00:00:12,407
A pbit chip only produces
random ones and zeros.

5
00:00:12,787 --> 00:00:17,117
How, then, does it represent
an activation with value 0.37?

6
00:00:17,497 --> 00:00:20,046
The idea is to use probability: the number

7
00:00:20,046 --> 00:00:22,474
is the frequency with which ones appear.

8
00:00:22,854 --> 00:00:27,076
Today we see how many effective bits
this yields and how much they cost.

9
00:00:28,183 --> 00:00:30,955
A probabilistic integer
is a random integer

10
00:00:30,955 --> 00:00:33,598
variable whose mean is the value we want.

11
00:00:33,978 --> 00:00:37,998
The simplest case is one pbit
with probability x of being one.

12
00:00:38,378 --> 00:00:41,255
With a group of n pbits, the sum takes

13
00:00:41,255 --> 00:00:44,434
values from zero to n and its mean is n x.

14
00:00:44,814 --> 00:00:48,438
In the syllabus, this family
of encodings appears as dy4p;

15
00:00:48,438 --> 00:00:52,061
here we study the general
principle, which does not depend

16
00:00:52,061 --> 00:00:54,810
on the details of a
specific implementation.

17
00:00:55,917 --> 00:01:00,080
We define effective bits by
comparing the mean squared error

18
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:04,313
with that of a uniform quantizer:
an error of one half to the

19
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:07,713
power b, divided by root
twelve, is worth b bits.

20
00:01:08,093 --> 00:01:12,916
If one sample gives b₁ effective
bits, averaging S samples divides the

21
00:01:12,916 --> 00:01:17,464
noise by root S, and that adds one
half of log base two of S bits.

22
00:01:18,583 --> 00:01:21,634
We verify it with twenty
thousand random values,

23
00:01:21,634 --> 00:01:24,875
encoded with stochastic
rounding to sixteen levels.

24
00:01:25,255 --> 00:01:31,166
One sample gives 3.42
effective bits; four samples,

25
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:36,844
4.41; sixteen, 5.41; two
hundred fifty-six, 7.41.

26
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:42,070
Each factor of four in samples adds
exactly one bit, just as the law predicts.

27
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:46,484
With a group of n binary
pbits the same happens: what

28
00:01:46,484 --> 00:01:49,286
counts is the total
number of samples, n · S.

29
00:01:49,666 --> 00:01:53,184
A single pbit with one
sample gives less than zero

30
00:01:53,184 --> 00:01:56,139
effective bits; with sixteen samples, 1.5.

31
00:01:56,519 --> 00:02:00,130
Sixteen pbits with
sixteen samples give 3.5.

32
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:05,139
One can spend in space, with more
pbits, or in time, with more samples.

33
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,412
This enables precision
on demand: each layer, or

34
00:02:09,412 --> 00:02:12,178
each token, can ask for the bits it needs.

35
00:02:12,558 --> 00:02:14,053
But the cost grows fast.

36
00:02:14,433 --> 00:02:17,972
Each additional bit requires
four times more samples, so

37
00:02:17,972 --> 00:02:21,512
energy grows as four to the
power of the number of bits.

38
00:02:21,892 --> 00:02:24,808
With few bits, probabilistic encoding is

39
00:02:24,808 --> 00:02:28,016
cheap; with many, a
digital multiplier wins.

40
00:02:28,396 --> 00:02:33,296
That is why TSU models are trained
to work with very few effective bits.

41
00:02:34,417 --> 00:02:37,797
In practice: encode activations
as pbit frequencies,

42
00:02:37,797 --> 00:02:41,697
distribute samples per layer
according to how much the error

43
00:02:41,697 --> 00:02:45,597
hurts, and train with the noise
included to need fewer bits.

44
00:02:45,977 --> 00:02:49,918
In lab twenty-nine you will
measure accuracy against samples.

45
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:54,934
Common mistakes: counting levels
as if they were effective bits.

46
00:02:55,314 --> 00:03:00,035
Expecting linear gains, when one more
bit costs four times more samples.

47
00:03:00,415 --> 00:03:04,210
Ignoring correlations between
samples, which reduce the gain.

48
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:07,617
And reporting bits without
saying how the error is defined.

49
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:13,041
In summary, a number is encoded
as the mean of pbit samples.

50
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:17,796
Effective bits follow the
law b₁ + ½ log₂ S, which we

51
00:03:17,796 --> 00:03:21,759
verified: four times more
samples, one more bit.

52
00:03:22,139 --> 00:03:25,083
Precision can be requested
on demand, but its

53
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:27,635
cost grows exponentially with the bits.

54
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:32,398
To practice: compute the
samples to gain three bits.

55
00:03:32,778 --> 00:03:35,880
Compute the variance of the
mean of a group of pbits.

56
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:39,047
Explain why pbits and samples trade off.

57
00:03:39,427 --> 00:03:41,790
And say when a digital multiplier wins.
